{
  "info": {
    "_postman_id": "b7c1e2a4-5f3d-4a91-9c88-relay000001",
    "name": "Relay",
    "description": "End-to-end collection for the Relay messenger.\n\n## Before you run\n\nStart infrastructure and services:\n\n```\ndocker compose up -d                     # keycloak, kafka, postgres x4, redis\n(cd eureka && ./gradlew bootRun)         # :8761\n(cd api-gateway && ./gradlew bootRun)    # :8080\n(cd auth && ./gradlew bootRun)           # random port, reached via the gateway\n(cd user && ./gradlew bootRun --args='--server.port=8085')\n(cd message && ./gradlew bootRun --args='--server.port=8084')\n(cd websocket-gateway && ./gradlew bootRun)  # :8083\n```\n\n`user` and `message` ship with `server.port: 0`, so they get a random port and are\nnormally reached only through Eureka. This collection calls their `/internal` endpoints\ndirectly, which needs a fixed port — hence the `--server.port` overrides above. Change the\n`userServiceUrl` / `messageServiceUrl` variables if you prefer different ones.\n\nKeycloak must have the `relay-realm` realm, a `relay-client` client with the secret in\n`auth/src/main/resources/application.yaml`, and a client role named `USER`.\n\n## How to run\n\nUse **Run collection** — the folders are ordered and later requests depend on variables\ncaptured by earlier ones (tokens, user ids, chat ids). Running requests one by one works\ntoo, as long as you go top to bottom.\n\n## WebSocket\n\nPostman collections cannot carry WebSocket requests, so the socket is not in here. Two ways\nto drive it:\n\n**1. Postman native WebSocket request** — New > WebSocket, URL `ws://localhost:8083/ws`.\nUnder the *Protocols* field add two entries: `access_token` and then your access token\nvalue. Connect, then send:\n\n```json\n{\"type\":\"PING\",\"nonce\":\"n-1\"}\n{\"type\":\"MESSAGE_SEND\",\"clientMessageId\":\"c-1\",\"chatId\":\"<chatId>\",\"body\":\"hello\"}\n```\n\n**2. Browser console** on any page:\n\n```js\nconst jwt = '<paste accessTokenA>';\nconst ws = new WebSocket('ws://localhost:8083/ws', ['access_token', jwt]);\nws.onmessage = e => console.log(e.data);\nws.onopen = () => ws.send(JSON.stringify({type:'PING', nonce:'n-1'}));\n// then:\n// ws.send(JSON.stringify({type:'MESSAGE_SEND', clientMessageId:crypto.randomUUID(), chatId:'<chatId>', body:'hi'}))\n```\n\nThe token goes in the subprotocol list rather than the URL, so it stays out of access logs\nand browser history. Expect `CONNECTED` first, then `MESSAGE_ACK` for a send, then\n`MESSAGE_NEW` pushed via Kafka (senders are among the recipients, so their own other\ndevices see the message too).\n\nTo watch delivery between two users, open a second socket as user B and send as user A.",
    "schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json"
  },
  "variable": [
    { "key": "gatewayUrl", "value": "http://localhost:8080", "type": "string" },
    { "key": "userServiceUrl", "value": "http://localhost:8085", "type": "string" },
    { "key": "messageServiceUrl", "value": "http://localhost:8084", "type": "string" },
    { "key": "wsGatewayUrl", "value": "http://localhost:8083", "type": "string" },
    { "key": "eurekaUrl", "value": "http://localhost:8761", "type": "string" },
    { "key": "keycloakUrl", "value": "http://localhost:8081", "type": "string" },
    { "key": "password", "value": "Relay!2345", "type": "string" },
    { "key": "emailA", "value": "", "type": "string" },
    { "key": "emailB", "value": "", "type": "string" },
    { "key": "accessTokenA", "value": "", "type": "string" },
    { "key": "refreshTokenA", "value": "", "type": "string" },
    { "key": "accessTokenB", "value": "", "type": "string" },
